WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.460
A secure gRPC service baseline starts by treating gRPC as an RPC framework, not a trust boundary.

2
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:15.640
The framework gives you structured contracts, generated clients, efficient serialization, and HTTP/2 communication.

3
00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:24.540
Your system still needs explicit identity, authorization, validation, resource controls, safe errors, and operational review.

4
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:29.700
Design narrow Protobuf contracts and enforce identity deliberately.

5
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:39.720
Messages should be purpose-driven, sensitive fields should be minimized, compatibility changes should be reviewed, and transport security should establish the service connection.

6
00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.380
Authentication identifies the caller.

7
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:50.480
Authorization decides which services, methods, tenants, records, and operations are allowed.

8
00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:53.400
Validate messages before work is performed.

9
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.860
Bound message types are not proof of business validity.

10
00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:07.040
Service methods should check required fields, ranges, formats, ownership, tenant scope, and method-specific rules before making changes or doing expensive work.

11
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:14.680
Bound resource use with message limits, streaming design review, deadlines, cancellation, and retry policy.

12
00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:18.220
These reliability features also protect capacity.

13
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:27.040
They help ensure that calls have time budgets, streams have controls, repeated attempts are safe, and canceled work does not continue without value.

14
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:38.120
Finally, review metadata, credentials, error details, observability, reflection, JSON transcoding, infrastructure, and production deployment.

15
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:47.820
Secure gRPC services are easier to operate when security decisions are visible from the contract through the channel, method, logs, and deployment path.
